difficulty of the problem.</p>
<p>It is therefore very important to be able to compare our model to at least one
alternative. In particular, we consider a simple <strong>baseline</strong>
model that is not very computationally expensive. Our neural network
should at least outperform this baseline model. If our network is not much
better than the baseline, then it is not doing well.</p>
<p>For our data imputation problem, consider the following baseline model:
to predict a missing feature, the baseline model will look at the <strong>most common value</strong> of the feature in the training set.</p>
<p>For example, if the feature "marriage" is missing, then this model's prediction will be the most common value for "marriage" in the training set, which happens to be "Married-civ-spouse".</p>
<p>What would be the test accuracy of this baseline model?</p>
